II. Binary Search
Definition
Binary Search is an efficient searching algorithm used to find a specific element in a sorted array. 
It works by repeatedly dividing the search range into two halves.
The middle element is compared with the element to be searched. If the middle element is equal to the target, the search is successful. 
If the target is smaller, the left half is searched; otherwise, the right half is searched. 
This process continues until the element is found or the search range becomes empty.
Time Complexity
Best Case: O(1)
Average Case: O(log n)
Worst Case: O(log n)
Auxiliary Space
The iterative implementation of Binary Search requires O(1) auxiliary space because it uses only a constant number of variables such as low, high, and mid.
Conclusion
Binary Search is an efficient searching algorithm for sorted arrays. By repeatedly dividing the search space into two halves, 
it significantly reduces the number of comparisons required to find an element. With a worst-case time complexity of O(log n) and O(1) auxiliary space in its
iterative implementation, Binary Search is highly suitable for searching large sorted datasets.

